--TEST--
An IPv6 address that carries an internal IPv4 address in its low 32 bits — IPv4-compatible or behind the NAT64 well-known prefix — is refused as the address it carries, and one carrying a public address is approved — `rule:security/net-address-policy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*: the same two prefixes
// carrying an internal address and a public one, on neighbouring lines, so a
// table that refused the whole prefix or none of it fails one half. Nothing
// here connects: `allowUrl` judges a literal address without a resolver.
array<string> $urls = [
    "http://[::127.0.0.1]/",
    "http://[64:ff9b::7f00:1]/",
    "http://[64:ff9b::169.254.169.254]/latest/meta-data/",
    "http://[::203.0.113.10]/",
    "http://[64:ff9b::203.0.113.10]/",
];
foreach ($urls as string $url) {
    try {
        Core\Http\Target $pinned = Core\Http::allowUrl($url);
        echo "approved ", $url, "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
        echo $refused->message, "\n";
    }
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.net]
connect = true
--EXPECT--
Core\Http::allowUrl refuses ::7f00:1: it is loopback (127.0.0.0/8), which `net.internal` does not except
Core\Http::allowUrl refuses 64:ff9b::7f00:1: it is loopback (127.0.0.0/8), which `net.internal` does not except
Core\Http::allowUrl refuses 64:ff9b::a9fe:a9fe: it is link-local (169.254.0.0/16), which `net.internal` does not except
approved http://[::203.0.113.10]/
approved http://[64:ff9b::203.0.113.10]/
